# sway config for the two-display kiosk.
#
# `cage` runs one fullscreen client and cannot place a second window on a
# second output — that is its whole design, and it is the right tool for the
# single-display fallback. Two displays need a compositor that can assign a
# window to an output, so the dual profile uses sway instead. It is still a
# tiling Wayland compositor with no desktop, no launcher and nothing that can
# steal focus; it simply knows about more than one screen.
#
# OUTPUT NAMES ARE MACHINE-SPECIFIC. Find yours with:
#
#   swaymsg -t get_outputs | grep -E '"name"|"model"'
#
# then set WM_KIOSK_OUT_DASHBOARD and WM_KIOSK_OUT_NAV in /etc/default/wm-kiosk.
# The `$out_*` values below are substituted by wm-kiosk-dual.sh at start; this
# file is a template, not a literal config.

### Outputs ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The console is the wider panel, so it sets the x origin and the dashboard is
# centred over it: (1424 - 1280) / 2 = 72. Getting this wrong does not show up
# as a layout bug — each window is fullscreen on its own output either way — it
# shows up as touch landing off by 72px on one panel, because pointer
# coordinates are in whole-layout space.
output $out_dashboard resolution 1280x400 position 72 0
output $out_nav resolution 1424x280 position 0 400

### Chrome ----------------------------------------------------------------

default_border none
default_floating_border none
titlebar_padding 0
gaps inner 0
gaps outer 0
# Nothing to focus-follow on a panel with no pointer, and a stray touch near an
# edge should not move focus between displays.
focus_follows_mouse no

# No wallpaper, no fallback colour that could flash between reloads: the field
# colour is the theme's job and the compositor should never be visible.
output * bg #000000 solid_color

### Window placement ------------------------------------------------------

# Each Chromium window is tagged with --class by wm-kiosk-launch.sh, which is
# what lets these rules put the right surface on the right panel. Without the
# class the two windows are indistinguishable and sway would place them by
# arrival order — a race that silently swaps the displays on a slow boot.
assign [app_id="wm-kiosk-dashboard"] output $out_dashboard
assign [app_id="wm-kiosk-nav"] output $out_nav

for_window [app_id="wm-kiosk-dashboard"] fullscreen enable, inhibit_idle focus
for_window [app_id="wm-kiosk-nav"] fullscreen enable, inhibit_idle focus

### Input -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Both panels are multi-touch. Left unmapped, a touchscreen sends its events in
# the coordinate space of the WHOLE layout, so a touch on the lower panel lands
# on the upper one. Mapping each device to its output is what makes touch work
# at all on a multi-output kiosk, and it is the single most common thing to get
# wrong here.
#
# Find device identifiers with: swaymsg -t get_inputs
input type:touch {
    events enabled
}

# Per-device mapping, substituted at start. Leave the env vars empty on a
# single-touchscreen setup.
input $in_touch_dashboard map_to_output $out_dashboard
input $in_touch_nav map_to_output $out_nav

### Start -----------------------------------------------------------------

exec /usr/local/bin/wm-kiosk-launch.sh dashboard
exec /usr/local/bin/wm-kiosk-launch.sh nav
